<h2>The proximity advantage, in practice</h2>
<p>Nowhere else do we ship as fast or as cheaply as South Asia. Bangladesh is reachable by sea in 2–5 days or overland in 1–3. Sri Lanka is 2–4 days by sea. Nepal, sourced from northern India, moves entirely by land in as little as a day. That translates into lower freight cost as a share of landed price, which is a large part of why India dominates staple-commodity supply into this region.</p>

<h2>What that means for buyers</h2>
<p>Fast, cheap transit supports genuine commodity trade at volume — onion, lentils, maize and spices moving in quantities that longer, costlier routes couldn't support economically. It also means South Asian buyers feel Indian supply disruptions (export policy changes especially) faster and more directly than almost anyone else, since there's little buffer inventory built into short-transit supply chains.</p>

<h2>Regional trade agreements</h2>
<p>SAFTA (South Asian Free Trade Area) and bilateral agreements like the India–Sri Lanka FTA and the India–Nepal Trade Treaty provide preferential tariff access for many qualifying goods across the region. We supply the origin documentation needed to support a claim; your customs agent confirms eligibility for your specific product.</p>
